Subject: Profile notes of freed shapes are deleted

What this does
  The profiler's slot-type notes (0567) delete a freed shape's note instead of marking it dead: its hash entry becomes a tombstone and the last note takes its place, so the notes array holds only live shapes. The value-shape set deletes too, and a table is rebuilt without its tombstones when it fills.

Why we need it
  A program that makes and frees shapes all the time grew the notes without bound, and every free of a value shape scanned all of them: the AOT corpus program alloc_literal_20 (an object literal of 20 properties per iteration, 82,263 iterations) ran for minutes under QJS_AOT_PROFILE_OUT, against 0.09 s plain; perf put 88% of the time in aot_note_shape_freed and 12% in aot_note_slot. It now takes 0.26 s.

Soundness
  The dump skipped dead notes, so deleting them drops nothing it read. A note's content is kept as it was; only its index moves, and the hash entry of the moved note is updated. A replay of 300,000 random note and free operations (with address reuse) through the old and the new code, under ASan and UBSan, gives the same note for every live shape (3,092 live; the old code held 48,969 notes, 45,877 of them dead). Profiles of real programs can differ in a few records because the tables' own allocations move where the engine's shapes land.

